  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para dir="ltr">The scatter plot shows the maximum noise level when different numbers of people are in a stadium. The linear model is given by the equation \(y = 1.5x + 22.7\), where \(y\) represents maximum noise level and \(x\) represents the number of people, in thousands, in the stadium.</para>
<para dir="ltr">\(y = 1.5x + 22.7\)</para>
<image alt="Scatter plot." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bf2fe432333358279dea1ef61899da13956c741b"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The slope of the linear model is 1.5. What does this mean in terms of the maximum noise level and the number of people?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>For every additional thousand people in the stadium, the noise level increases by about 1.5 decibels.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>A sports announcer states that there are 65,000 fans in the stadium. Estimate the maximum noise level. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>120.2 decibels</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Is this estimate reasonable?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>It is a reasonable value since the data seem to fit a linear model well, so it is probably near this value.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the \(y\)-intercept of the linear model given?  Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The 𝑦-intercept is (0, 22.7).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What does the value of the \(y\)-intercept mean in the context of the problem?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The point (0, 22.7) means a stadium with no people in it will have a maximum noise level of 22.7 decibels.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Is this value for the \(y\)-intercept reasonable?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item> This is actually reasonable since a whisper is about 20 decibels. </item>
      <item> This is not reasonable since it should be silent with no people in the stadium. </item>
      <item> This is not reasonable because the point is so far from the data that it is unlikely that the linear model will be accurate. </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
